ncan Smith said it  wasn't a one nation budget because of  those cuts for middle income earners  which is part of your manifesto because  that was then balanced on one side by  these further cuts to disability  benefits  so I am being filmed in the grounds of  my mother-in-law's house and she does  not need to receive a 200 pound per year  winter fuel allowance Iain Duncan Smith  has always been denied any suggestion  that he could start raining back on some  of these very generous benefits to  better off pensioners he's always been  told these cuts have got to bear on  working families people trying to get  back into work disabled people who want  to work now just ask yourself should we  be protecting rich pensioners in order  to be able to cut benefits for people  who are trying to do the right thing and  struggling with their lives and their  families this has got out of balance and  he's been struggling with this balance  for some time and frankly for the  government to keep saying oh were very  perplexed we don't understand why in  Duncan Smith has resigned I mean they  are deliberately trying to fog the  atmosphere it is deeply insincere for  them to pretend they don't know why in  Duncan Smith has resigned and it is just  don't want to hear they cannot hear and  I think the the highest level point on  this is about how Downing Street  operates it is they've just taken the  same playbook as brown and Blair  everything is dictated from the top for  short-term political Vantage everything  is tactical this cannot go on and the  point in Duncan Smith made today about  cabinet government and Jacob rees-mogg  has made the same point I've written  about it in the observer today we need  to reset how Whitehall operates you have  government departments with secretaries  of state running them because they  should have responsibility and the  Chancellor and the Prime Minister are  just part of a team the Prime Minister  is meant to be primos into Paris he's  not meant t